<h2>Three kinds of question</h2>

<p>Each unit page carries three questions under its sentence, and they are labeled on purpose. They are not three attempts at the same question. They are a route.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Factual.</strong> Has a findable answer. You can look it up, and you should. This is where the vocabulary and the mechanisms live.</li>
  <li><strong>Conceptual.</strong> Asks why the facts behave the way they do. The answer transfers to a case you have not met.</li>
  <li><strong>Debatable.</strong> Informed people who know the evidence still disagree. Your job is not to find the answer but to hold a position and say what would change your mind.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you can only answer the factual one, you know the unit's content. If you can answer all three, you have the unit. The <a href="/foundations/the-rubric/">rubric</a> reflects that: K/U rewards the first, T/T rewards the second and third, and C rewards whether any of it reaches a reader.</p>

